WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.480
One street, three minutes end to end, and the world's word for made-to-measure.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.109
A house you could not buy into: you had to be elected.

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:19.208
Every road sign in Britain that says LONDON means this spot.

00:00:19.488 --> 00:00:23.797
Six weeks before this story starts, the last gap in the wire closed.

00:00:24.077 --> 00:00:29.257
That autumn a novelist bet that the newly wired world could be circled in eighty

00:00:29.257 --> 00:00:29.581
days.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.376
This is the whole subject: two hundred and eighty metres,

00:00:55.376 --> 00:00:57.568
and you can walk it in three minutes.

00:00:57.848 --> 00:00:59.960
One block behind Regent Street.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.739
Built in the seventeen-thirties as ordinary houses.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.982
Nobody planned a tailoring street.

00:01:06.262 --> 00:01:08.203
Nothing about the street is special.

00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:09.742
The customers were.

00:01:10.022 --> 00:01:14.935
Tailors were one street west, dressing the gentry, and they followed them in

00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:18.491
— a trade cluster, the way Silicon Valley is a cluster.

00:01:18.771 --> 00:01:25.504
The root here is uniform, not fashion: an army cap-maker of 1771,

00:01:25.504 --> 00:01:28.094
a navy outfitter of 1785.

00:01:28.374 --> 00:01:31.913
That shoulder was cut for men who had to ride, march,

00:01:31.913 --> 00:01:34.049
and be recognised at a distance.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:39.598
Every suit starts as chalk on cloth, cut from a paper pattern made for one person.

00:01:39.878 --> 00:01:41.990
From seven and a half thousand pounds.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.494
An apprentice spends two to six years learning one part of the job.

00:01:46.774 --> 00:01:49.867
Six years before anyone trusts you with trousers.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.005
Where does the word bespoke come from?

00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:35.210
The word is about fifty years older than the first tailor on this street.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:40.226
The trade tells it differently: cloth spoken for by a customer, right here.

00:02:40.506 --> 00:02:41.786
The dates say no.

00:02:42.066 --> 00:02:47.686
In June two thousand and eight the Row took a cheaper rival to the advertising

00:02:47.686 --> 00:02:52.945
regulator: four-hundred-and-ninety-five-pound suits, machine-sewn abroad,

00:02:52.945 --> 00:02:54.098
sold as bespoke.

00:02:54.378 --> 00:02:55.509
The Row lost.

00:02:55.789 --> 00:03:00.632
Bespoke and made-to-measure, the regulator said, now mean much the same thing.

00:03:00.912 --> 00:03:02.555
The other pressure is rent.

00:03:02.835 --> 00:03:06.171
A chain store opened at number three in 2013;

00:03:06.171 --> 00:03:11.731
in 2016 the council wrote planning protection for tailoring on this street.

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:16.727
So they stopped defending the word and defined the thing — by geography:

00:03:16.727 --> 00:03:19.478
made within a hundred yards of Savile Row.

00:03:19.758 --> 00:03:23.001
A hundred and sixty-nine firms in 1912.

00:03:23.281 --> 00:03:25.542
Nineteen by 2006.

00:03:25.822 --> 00:03:32.060
Twenty-two houses today — and the Row's first woman head cutter in 2009,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.313
with her own shop by 2016.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.307
At the bottom of the street: number one, Savile Row.

00:04:16.587 --> 00:04:21.371
From 1870 to 1912 this was the Royal Geographical Society:

00:04:21.371 --> 00:04:27.723
the room where British expeditions to Africa, Asia and the Pole were planned.

00:04:28.003 --> 00:04:34.104
David Livingstone's body lay here in April 1874, before Westminster Abbey.

00:04:34.384 --> 00:04:39.395
And on this same two hundred and eighty metres, in a novel published that autumn,

00:04:39.395 --> 00:04:41.808
a man is packing to go round the world.

00:04:42.088 --> 00:04:44.947
The premise is an argument, not a character.

00:04:45.227 --> 00:04:49.372
A newspaper table in the book says eighty days is now possible,

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:53.056
and writers had been making that case since Suez opened.

00:04:53.336 --> 00:04:55.619
A bet, half a fortune, eighty days.

00:04:55.899 --> 00:05:01.374
In 1912 the military tailor at number one bought the explorers' headquarters for

00:05:01.374 --> 00:05:06.917
thirty-eight thousand pounds — partly because the firm had dressed so many of the

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.723
men who planned their journeys inside it.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.200
This walk is a Victorian gentleman's supply chain.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.576
Fogg walks it every day at half past eleven, counting his steps.

00:05:55.856 --> 00:05:56.944
Half a mile.

00:05:57.224 --> 00:05:59.443
Everything in this film happens on it.

00:05:59.723 --> 00:06:04.198
Henry the Eighth built the palace at the bottom in 1536;

00:06:04.198 --> 00:06:09.472
it became the monarch's London home when Whitehall burned in 1698.

00:06:09.752 --> 00:06:11.565
The quarter grew up to serve it.

00:06:11.845 --> 00:06:16.218
The world's oldest hat shop has been here since 1676.

00:06:16.498 --> 00:06:19.122
A wine merchant since 1698.

00:06:19.402 --> 00:06:21.834
A bootmaker since 1866.

00:06:22.114 --> 00:06:27.041
Every ambassador to Britain is still accredited to the Court of St James's,

00:06:27.041 --> 00:06:30.391
a palace no monarch has lived in for two centuries.

00:06:30.671 --> 00:06:32.079
Clothes, then club.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.240
Three doors in a row on Pall Mall.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:03.971
Three institutions.

00:07:04.251 --> 00:07:09.513
This is what a club gave you: a man with a modest London address got the run of a

00:07:09.513 --> 00:07:09.968
palace.

00:07:10.248 --> 00:07:13.768
Dining room, library, billiards, bedrooms, servants.

00:07:14.048 --> 00:07:17.299
You could eat, read, write, take your post and sleep,

00:07:17.299 --> 00:07:19.936
without letting anyone into your own house.

00:07:20.216 --> 00:07:21.517
You did not apply.

00:07:21.797 --> 00:07:27.103
You were proposed and seconded, then the membership voted with balls dropped in a

00:07:27.103 --> 00:07:27.365
box.

00:07:27.645 --> 00:07:31.144
Black balls kept you out — that is where the word comes from.

00:07:31.424 --> 00:07:36.672
Churchill resigned from this club in 1913 because a friend was blackballed.

00:07:36.952 --> 00:07:41.176
At their height there were hundreds of them, packed into a few streets;

00:07:41.176 --> 00:07:44.269
half the principal ones founded within thirty years.

00:07:44.549 --> 00:07:49.158
Next door, the Travellers — founded in 1819, in its own words,

00:07:49.158 --> 00:07:52.058
for gentlemen who had travelled abroad.

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:56.941
Your club announced your politics: the Tory club at one end of the street,

00:07:56.941 --> 00:07:58.994
the reformers' three doors along.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.008
Budgeted at thirty-seven and a half thousand pounds.

00:08:51.288 --> 00:08:53.016
It finished at eighty-two.

00:08:53.296 --> 00:08:55.900
Six floors, three of them below the street

00:08:55.900 --> 00:09:00.549
— kitchens and the engine that heats the whole house underneath everything.

00:09:00.829 --> 00:09:05.835
In the eighteen-thirties a club did the work a party headquarters does now:

00:09:05.835 --> 00:09:09.106
registering voters, whipping, picking candidates.

00:09:09.386 --> 00:09:14.822
This one opened with a thousand members, nearly two hundred and fifty of them MPs,

00:09:14.822 --> 00:09:17.407
to counter the Tory club down the road.

00:09:17.687 --> 00:09:20.396
A celebrity chef in 1837.

00:09:20.676 --> 00:09:25.476
Alexis Soyer helped design these kitchens, then published them as a book.

00:09:25.756 --> 00:09:27.655
People came to tour a basement.

00:09:27.935 --> 00:09:32.816
A members' library of eighty-five thousand volumes, all of it donated

00:09:32.816 --> 00:09:36.212
— which is why a member needed no study at home.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.205
A card table at the Reform, an afternoon in October 1872.

00:10:07.485 --> 00:10:10.578
A newspaper says eighty days is now possible.

00:10:10.858 --> 00:10:12.650
One of the players says it lies.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.703
Eight legs, and the days land as they go.

00:10:15.983 --> 00:10:17.434
They add up to eighty.

00:10:17.714 --> 00:10:20.381
Twenty thousand pounds — half his fortune.

00:10:20.661 --> 00:10:24.672
Back in this room on the twenty-first of December, a quarter to nine.

00:10:24.952 --> 00:10:26.083
Eighty days.

00:10:26.363 --> 00:10:28.624
Nineteen hundred and twenty hours.

00:10:28.904 --> 00:10:31.805
A hundred and fifteen thousand two hundred minutes.

00:10:32.085 --> 00:10:35.830
Fogg's bank lasted two hundred and thirty-three years

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:41.696
— until one trader lost eight hundred and twenty-seven million pounds and killed it

00:10:41.696 --> 00:10:42.261
in 1995.

00:10:42.541 --> 00:10:47.170
Verne gives him one line worth keeping: the unforeseen does not exist.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:52.698
And the turn, in Verne's words: the train leaves for Dover at a quarter before nine.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.949
Why was 1872 the first year anyone could seriously try this?

00:11:37.229 --> 00:11:40.109
Because of this: the largest port in the world.

00:11:40.389 --> 00:11:46.597
Eight hundred thousand tons of cargo a year in 1800; eight million by 1880.

00:11:46.877 --> 00:11:50.482
Every steamer timetable in the story radiates from here.

00:11:50.762 --> 00:11:53.066
This film is thirty-one years too late.

00:11:53.346 --> 00:11:58.729
There is no moving picture of 1872 — this is the Strand in 1903,

00:11:58.729 --> 00:12:02.093
and it is the closest thing that exists.

00:12:02.373 --> 00:12:04.208
Close to four million people.

00:12:04.488 --> 00:12:06.195
A quarter more than Beijing.

00:12:06.475 --> 00:12:09.035
The river wall he crosses was two years old.

00:12:09.315 --> 00:12:15.630
In 1878 it became the first street in Britain permanently lit by electricity.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:19.387
Twelve main-line termini by 1865.

00:12:19.667 --> 00:12:22.867
No other city on earth had anything like that many.

00:12:23.147 --> 00:12:26.743
The world's first underground railway was nine years old,

00:12:26.743 --> 00:12:30.592
and carried nine and a half million people in its first year.

00:12:30.872 --> 00:12:36.300
Between 1870 and 1872 they ran a wire up the middle of Australia

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:41.219
— thirty-six thousand poles — to meet the cable from Java.

00:12:41.499 --> 00:12:45.467
It was finished on the twenty-second of August 1872.

00:12:45.747 --> 00:12:50.398
From London that October you could telegraph almost anywhere a steamer called.

00:12:50.678 --> 00:12:52.193
He races the ships.

00:12:52.473 --> 00:12:54.926
The news about him travels instantly.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.963
This is the middle of London.

00:13:52.243 --> 00:13:53.246
Officially.

00:13:53.526 --> 00:13:58.006
When a road sign in Scotland says LONDON, it means this bronze horse.

00:13:58.286 --> 00:14:02.062
Charing is Old English: cierring, a bend in the road.

00:14:02.342 --> 00:14:06.374
Not chère reine — the name is a century older than the queen.

00:14:06.654 --> 00:14:11.518
Eleanor of Castile died in Nottinghamshire in November 1290.

00:14:11.798 --> 00:14:16.589
Her husband raised a stone cross at every place her body rested on the road to

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:19.414
Westminster, so travellers would pray for her.

00:14:19.694 --> 00:14:23.385
The last night before the Abbey, so the biggest of the twelve.

00:14:23.665 --> 00:14:27.612
Parliament pulled it down, and the parish sold the stone for paving.

00:14:27.892 --> 00:14:31.755
That bronze was cast in 1633, ordered destroyed,

00:14:31.755 --> 00:14:36.020
hidden by the brazier told to melt it, and sold back.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.436
It faces where Charles the First was executed.

00:14:39.716 --> 00:14:43.762
The cross in the station yard is an 1865 replica,

00:14:43.762 --> 00:14:46.735
two hundred yards off the real spot.

00:14:47.015 --> 00:14:50.066
Brunel's footbridge came down for the railway bridge.

00:14:50.346 --> 00:14:52.951
Its chains sold for five thousand pounds,

00:14:52.951 --> 00:14:56.383
and finished the Clifton Suspension Bridge at Bristol.

00:14:56.663 --> 00:14:58.071
Why a station here?

00:14:58.351 --> 00:15:02.916
The company's only London terminus was on the wrong side of the river.

00:15:03.196 --> 00:15:07.996
This one opened in 1864, eight years old the night Fogg leaves.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:16.520
Almost no luggage — a fifth of his fortune in cash.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:23.473
That red book is a Bradshaw: the world's first compiled railway timetable, 1839,

00:16:23.473 --> 00:16:24.224
sixpence.

00:16:24.504 --> 00:16:30.576
By 1872, hundreds of pages of trains and steamers: the internet of the age,

00:16:30.576 --> 00:16:31.629
in a satchel.

00:16:31.909 --> 00:16:37.434
A hansom: two wheels, the driver perched behind and above you, a hatch in the roof.

00:16:37.714 --> 00:16:39.037
Sixpence a mile.

00:16:39.317 --> 00:16:43.520
He gives her the twenty guineas he won at cards, without breaking stride.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:49.283
Four hundred and twenty shillings: eight hundred miles of cab at sixpence a mile.

00:16:49.563 --> 00:16:53.470
A sum, not a coin: a price that outlived its own money,

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.240
and still how Savile Row quotes a suit.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:58.120
Tonight it simply rains.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.176
The famous pea-soupers were winter coal-smoke, not weather;

00:17:02.176 --> 00:17:05.888
the worst killed prize cattle at Smithfield the next year.

00:17:06.168 --> 00:17:11.416
About thirteen hundred gas lamps still burn in London, tended by lamplighters.

00:17:11.696 --> 00:17:15.813
Past New Cross the valet remembers the gas burning in his room.

00:17:16.093 --> 00:17:19.464
It will burn for eighty days, at his master's expense.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:12.621
One thing to take away.

00:18:12.901 --> 00:18:16.302
The next time you pass a sign that says LONDON and a number,

00:18:16.302 --> 00:18:18.853
you will know exactly what is being measured.

00:18:19.133 --> 00:18:24.381
Tomorrow: Dover and Calais — twenty miles of water, and why both sides are white.
